<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="4-aminobenzoate biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6543" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: P-AMINO-BENZOATE &quot;4-Aminobenzoate&quot;| is an important metabolic intermediate, leading to the biosynthesis of various products such as |FRAME: All-Folates folates|, |FRAME: Ubiquinones ubiquinone| (in eukaryotes), and various bacterial antibiotics (e.g. |FRAME: Candicidins|). In this pathway |FRAME: P-AMINO-BENZOATE| is produced from |FRAME: CHORISMATE| in two steps. In the first step |FRAME: EC-2.6.1.85|, synthesizes |FRAME: 4-AMINO-4-DEOXYCHORISMATE| from |FRAME: CHORISMATE| and |FRAME: GLN|. The enzyme is composed from two subunit, PabA and PabB (in some organisms they are fused together into a bifunctional protein). PabA functions as a glutaminase, but only when in complex with PabB. PabB is an aminodeoxychorismate synthase that utilizes chorismate and ammonia (derived from glutamine hydrolysis by PabA) as substrates. In the absence of PabA PabB can utilize added ammonia at a reduced rate |CITS: [2251281][AndersonK91][1637823]|. During the reaction chorismate binds to PabB by a transitory covalent bond between the &amp;epsilon;-amino group of a lysine residue (K274 in the enzyme from |FRAME: TAX-582|) and position C-2 of chorismate, resulting in loss of the hydroxyl group at position C-4. This intermediate is then attacked by the ammonia molecule generated by PabA, resulting in release of the product |FRAME: 4-AMINO-4-DEOXYCHORISMATE| |CITS: [15770625][16605270]|. In the second step |FRAME: EC-4.1.3.38| (PabC) forms the final product. The reaction involves the elimination of pyruvate from 4-amino-4-deoxychorismate with concomitant aromatization of the cyclohexadiene ring. The enzyme is classified as an &amp;alpha;,&amp;beta;-lyase of the fold-type IV family, and requires pyridoxal 5&apos;-phosphate for activity |CITS: [11011151]|.</Description>
